    <body>
      <sec id="article-25669.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Neonatal lupus erythematosus (NLE) is a rare autoimmune disorder resulting from the passive transplacental transfer of anti-La/SSB and anti-Ro/SSA antibodies, affecting approximately 2% of at-risk pregnancies and with&#x000a0;a recurrence risk of up to 20%. This activity provides a comprehensive review of NLE, emphasizing its role as the leading cause of severe congenital atrioventricular block in the neonatal period and addressing its transient cutaneous, hepatic, and hematologic manifestations. Participants will enhance their ability to identify at-risk pregnancies, interpret maternal and neonatal serologic findings, and implement evidence-based fetal surveillance strategies, including serial echocardiography. The activity also highlights the importance of maternal counseling, preventive strategies such as hydroxychloroquine use, and individualized neonatal management. Through an interprofessional approach, learners will strengthen coordination across specialties to support early diagnosis, optimize outcomes, and improve long-term neonatal health.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify&#x000a0;pregnancies at risk for neonatal lupus erythematosis based on maternal autoantibody status and clinical history.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Screen pregnant patients appropriately for anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB antibodies.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Select the most appropriate immunosuppressive or anti-inflammatory therapy based on maternal and fetal status.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate within the interprofessional team to integrate maternal-fetal medicine, neonatology, pediatric cardiology, rheumatology, and nursing expertise for optimal outcomes.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=25669&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=25669">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25669.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Neonatal lupus erythematosus (NLE) is a passively acquired autoimmune disorder of the fetus and neonate that results from the transplacental transfer of maternal immunoglobulin G (IgG) autoantibodies during pregnancy. The antibodies most commonly implicated are directed against the Sj&#x000f6;gren syndrome&#x02013;associated ribonucleoprotein antigens Ro/SSA and La/SSB, although additional maternal autoantibodies may occasionally be involved.</p>
        <p>Through Fc receptor&#x02013;mediated placental transport, these IgG antibodies enter the fetal circulation and bind to specific fetal tissues, leading to immune-mediated injury and a characteristic spectrum of clinical manifestations. The pathogenesis of NLE exemplifies the broader phenomenon of passive neonatal autoimmunity, in which maternal autoantibodies transiently affect fetal or neonatal organ systems without the infant's endogenous antibody production.</p>
        <p>This mechanism parallels that observed in other antibody-mediated neonatal disorders, including neonatal antiphospholipid antibody syndrome, Graves disease, immune thrombocytopenic purpura, myasthenia gravis, and autoimmune blistering dermatoses. These conditions arise from placental transfer of pathogenic maternal IgG antibodies and typically resolve as maternal antibodies are gradually cleared from the neonatal circulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>NLE was first described in 1954 by Bridge and Foley, who documented the transplacental transfer of the maternal lupus erythematosus factor and its association with clinical manifestations in infants. That same year, a lupus-associated cutaneous eruption was described in a 6-week-old infant whose mother was subsequently diagnosed with systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE).</p>
        <p>In 1957, congenital heart block was reported in an infant born to a mother with SLE, further supporting the association between maternal autoimmunity and neonatal disease. As clinical recognition of NLE&#x000a0;expanded, the disorder came to be understood as a multisystem condition, with its clinical spectrum now recognized to include not only cutaneous and cardiac manifestations but also hepatic enzyme abnormalities and transient cytopenias.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r5">[5]</xref></p>
        <p>The clinical manifestations of&#x000a0;NLE encompass a broad spectrum, ranging from transient hematologic and cutaneous abnormalities to severe and potentially irreversible cardiac complications, most notably congenital heart block. Importantly, a considerable proportion of affected neonates are born to mothers without clinically overt autoimmune disease, underscoring the significance of maternal autoantibody screening during pregnancy to facilitate early identification and monitoring of at-risk pregnancies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r7">[7]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25669.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>NLE&#x000a0;arises from the passive transplacental transfer of maternal autoantibodies against Anti-Ro/SSA and Anti-La/SSB to the developing fetus. Anti-Ro/SSA antibodies recognize&#x000a0;2 principal ribonucleoprotein antigens: Ro52, which is localized in both the nucleus and cytoplasm, and Ro60, which is predominantly localized in the nucleus and nucleolus. In contrast, anti-La/SSB antibodies are directed against a 47-kDa ribonucleoprotein that shuttles between the nuclear and cytoplasmic compartments. Although a definitive pathogenic epitope has not been clearly established, experimental and animal model data suggest that antibodies targeting Ro52 may play a particularly prominent role in the pathogenesis of this disorder.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25669.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>NLE&#x000a0;is an uncommon condition, with an estimated incidence of approximately 1 in 12,500 to 20,000 live births.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r8">[8]</xref> Among infants born to mothers who carry Anti-Ro/SSA antibodies or Anti-La/SSB antibodies, the risk of developing NLE is approximately 2%, with a recurrence risk of 18% to 20% in subsequent pregnancies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r9">[9]</xref></p>
        <p>These autoantibodies are detectable in roughly 0.1% to 1.5% of otherwise healthy pregnant women, but they occur far more frequently in the setting of systemic autoimmune disease. They are identified in up to 90% of patients with Sj&#x000f6;gren Syndrome, 20% to 30% of those with SLE, and approximately 3% to 4% of individuals with rheumatoid arthritis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <p>At the time of delivery, approximately 25% of mothers of affected infants are clinically asymptomatic, while an additional 25% exhibit only nonspecific or undifferentiated autoimmune features.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r10">[10]</xref>&#x000a0;Nevertheless, longitudinal follow-up studies indicate that nearly half of initially asymptomatic mothers subsequently develop a defined systemic autoimmune disorder, most commonly&#x000a0;SLE or Sj&#x000f6;gren Syndrome, typically within the first&#x000a0;3 years postpartum.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r13">[13]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r15">[15]</xref></p>
        <p>Cardiac involvement represents the most serious manifestation of NLE. It accounts for approximately 80% to 95% of cases of severe congenital atrioventricular (AV) block presenting during&#x000a0;the neonatal period. In contrast, it accounts for only about 5% of&#x000a0;AV block cases diagnosed later in childhood.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Among mothers who have previously delivered an affected child, the risk of recurrence in subsequent pregnancies is estimated at approximately 18%, corresponding to a 6 to 10 times&#x000a0;increase in risk compared with the baseline incidence.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r17">[17]</xref></p>
        <p>Cutaneous manifestations of&#x000a0;NLE occur in approximately 7% to 16% of infants born to mothers with Anti-Ro/SSA or Anti-La/SSB antibodies, with lesions most commonly appearing after birth rather than during the prenatal period. Less commonly, isolated cutaneous NLE without cardiac involvement has been described in infants born to mothers with Anti-U1 RNP antibodies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r18">[18]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r19">[19]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25669.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>In NLE, fibrosis of the fetal cardiac conduction system, particularly the AV node, underlies the development of congenital AV block. The leading pathogenic hypothesis posits that maternal anti-Ro/SSA antibodies trigger an inflammatory cascade within fetal cardiac tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r16">[16]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r20">[20]</xref>&#x000a0;This injury typically occurs between 18 and 24 weeks of gestation, when SSA/Ro antigens are expressed on the surface of fetal cardiomyocytes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r16">[16]</xref></p>
        <p>Histopathologic examination demonstrates replacement of the AV node and adjacent myocardium by fibrous tissue and calcifications, frequently accompanied by dense infiltration of macrophages and T lymphocytes. A complementary mechanism involves molecular mimicry between the Ro antigen and cardiac L-type calcium channels.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r22">[22]</xref> Maternal anti-Ro antibodies may bind to these channels, disrupting calcium flux essential for normal electrical conduction through the AV and sinoatrial nodes.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r23">[23]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r24">[24]</xref> This process may account for conduction abnormalities observed before the development of overt fibrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r24">[24]</xref></p>
        <p>Higher maternal autoantibody titers are associated with an increased risk of fetal AV block. Cardiac involvement in NLE may extend beyond the conduction system. Endocardial fibroelastosis, characterized by thickening of the ventricular endocardium due to collagen and elastin deposition, can lead to intrauterine myocarditis and has been reported in some cases, often in conjunction with complete heart block.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r25">[25]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r26">[26]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r27">[27]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r28">[28]</xref></p>
        <p>Other manifestations of NLE&#x02014;including cutaneous eruptions, hepatobiliary abnormalities, and hematologic cytopenias&#x02014;are generally transient, resolving spontaneously within the first few months of life as maternal autoantibodies are cleared from the neonatal circulation. The pathogenesis of cutaneous NLE is primarily mediated by maternal antibody deposition at the dermal-epidermal junction. Histopathologic examination of active skin lesions typically reveals a lupus-like pattern, characterized by IgG and C3 complement deposition along the basement membrane zone and interface dermatitis. In some cases, maternal anti-U1 RNP antibodies (directed against the small nuclear ribonucleoprotein associated with U1 spliceosomal RNA) have been implicated in the development of cutaneous lesions.</p>
        <p>Hepatic involvement may manifest as mild lupus-like hepatitis, with histology showing lymphocytic infiltration of the portal tracts and hepatic parenchyma. Hematologic abnormalities, including neutropenia and thrombocytopenia, are thought to result from passive transfer of maternal anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB antibodies, which can bind to fetal blood cell antigens or form immune complexes, ultimately leading to peripheral destruction of blood cells.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r3">[3]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25669.s6" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>The clinical presentation of&#x000a0;NLE is heterogeneous, involving the cutaneous, cardiac, hepatobiliary, and hematologic systems, and, less commonly, the central nervous system. Maternal evaluation should include a detailed history of previous pregnancies affected by NLE, established autoimmune disease, or documented positivity for anti-Ro/SSA or anti-La/SSB antibodies. Although the majority of manifestations are self-limited, a notable subset of affected infants develops irreversible cardiac conduction abnormalities, representing the most distinctive and clinically significant complication of the disorder.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r29">[29]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Cutaneous Manifestations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Characteristic cutaneous lesions are observed in up to 40% of infants with NLE, typically appearing within the first few weeks of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r30">[30]</xref> These eruptions are frequently misattributed to birth trauma, infection, or eczema, particularly when the mother is asymptomatic. Lesions are often absent or subtle at birth and may emerge after exposure to sunlight or ultraviolet light.</p>
        <p>Clinically,&#x000a0;lesions present as annular or elliptical erythematous plaques with raised borders and central clearing, most commonly affecting the face, especially the periorbital region, producing the classic &#x0201c;raccoon-eye&#x0201d; appearance in approximately 95% of cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r32">[32]</xref>&#x000a0;Other commonly involved sites include the scalp (60%), the trunk (with or without groin involvement) (25%), the extremities (25%), and, rarely, generalized involvement (10%).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r33">[33]</xref></p>
        <p>Cutaneous manifestations are generally self-limited, with resolution occurring within 4 months in roughly 50% of cases, by 7 months in 80% of cases, and within the first year in all affected infants. Nonetheless, approximately 20% of neonates may develop persistent skin changes, including telangiectasia, hyperpigmentation, and atrophic scarring.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r36">[36]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Cardiac Manifestations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Infants with NLE generally have a structurally normal heart at birth. Cardiac involvement occurs in approximately 25% of cases and represents the most distinctive and clinically significant manifestation of the disorder.</p>
        <p>It most commonly presents as congenital AV block, which may be first-, second-, or, most frequently, third-degree. This conduction abnormality is typically identified in utero between 18 and 24 weeks of gestation, often manifesting as fetal bradycardia with ventricular rates of 40 to 80 beats per minute.</p>
        <p>Additional cardiac manifestations in NLE may include sinus bradycardia, QT-interval prolongation, cardiomyopathy, congestive heart failure, and myocarditis. Structural or valvular abnormalities have also been reported, including ventricular septal defects, ostium secundum&#x02013;type atrial septal defects, patent foramen ovale, patent ductus arteriosus, pulmonary stenosis, pulmonary valve dysplasia, and fusion of the tricuspid valve chordae tendineae.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r37">[37]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r38">[38]</xref></p>
        <p>Neonates affected by NLE may exhibit persistent bradycardia or signs of congestive heart failure, including poor feeding, diaphoresis, pallor, peripheral edema, lethargy, tachypnea, hepatomegaly, jugular venous distension, and pulmonary crackles. Cardiac auscultation may reveal a slow, regular heart rate, variable S1 intensity, or a fixed split S2. Approximately 5% to 10% of infants present with severe AV block, which can lead to myocardial dysfunction secondary to endocardial fibroelastosis and myocardial fibrosis. These structural and conduction abnormalities may necessitate right ventricular pacing, which can further contribute to ventricular dyssynchrony and compromised cardiac function.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r37">[37]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r39">[39]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Hematological and Hepatobiliary Involvement</bold>
</p>
        <p>Hematologic abnormalities in NLE commonly include anemia, neutropenia, and thrombocytopenia, which are generally mild, self-limited, and transient, resolving as maternal autoantibodies are cleared from the neonatal circulation. More severe manifestations, such as aplastic anemia, have been documented in approximately 20% of affected infants, highlighting the potential for significant, though uncommon, bone marrow suppression.</p>
        <p>Hepatic involvement is reported in 15% to 25% of cases and may present with a spectrum of clinical and laboratory findings, including asymptomatic elevations in serum aminotransferases, cholestasis, hepatomegaly, or splenomegaly. These hepatic abnormalities are typically transient, resolving over several months as pathogenic maternal antibodies decline. Histopathologic evaluation, when performed, may demonstrate lymphocytic infiltration and mild hepatocellular injury, consistent with a lupus-like pattern of hepatic involvement.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r40">[40]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r41">[41]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Neurological Involvement</bold>
</p>
        <p>Central nervous system involvement in NLE is infrequent. When present, the most characteristic manifestation is benign, transient hydrocephalus, which may lead to macrocephaly during the neonatal period.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r33">[33]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25669.s7" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>When NLE is suspected or diagnosed, a comprehensive evaluation should be undertaken to assess for systemic involvement, including the cardiac, cutaneous, hepatic, hematologic, and, when clinically indicated, neurologic systems.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Laboratory Evaluation</bold>
</p>
        <p>The diagnosis of NLE relies on detecting maternal autoantibodies, either in the mother&#x02014;if her serologic status is unknown&#x02014;or in the neonate, in whom transplacentally acquired antibodies persist for 6 to 8 months. Testing should, at a minimum, include anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB antibodies. In cases where these are negative but clinical suspicion remains high&#x02014;particularly for isolated cutaneous manifestations&#x02014;anti-U1 ribonucleoprotein antibody testing may be considered. Additional laboratory assessments may include a complete blood count to evaluate for cytopenias, liver function tests, and inflammatory markers, though these are often normal or nonspecific.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Electrocardiography</bold>
</p>
        <p>All infants with suspected NLE should undergo electrocardiographic (ECG) evaluation to identify AV conduction abnormalities, such as prolonged PR interval, bundle branch block, or other rhythm disturbances. A normal ECG at birth is generally reassuring. However, in infants with a history of in utero first- or second-degree AV block that subsequently resolved, serial ECG monitoring during the neonatal period is recommended, as late progression to higher-degree block can occur. The risk of AV block is notably elevated in infants born to mothers with a prior NLE-associated cardiac manifestation.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Echocardiography</bold>
</p>
        <p>Echocardiography is essential for both fetal surveillance and postnatal assessment. In at-risk pregnancies&#x02014;defined by maternal anti-Ro/SSA or anti-La/SSB positivity&#x02014;serial fetal echocardiograms are recommended beginning at approximately 16 weeks&#x02019; gestation. Fewer than 20% of cases are detected after 26 weeks, allowing for a reduction in screening frequency if prior studies remain normal. Fetal echocardiography can evaluate conduction delays via Doppler measurement of the mechanical PR interval and identify early indicators of heart block or myocarditis, including fetal bradycardia, atrial echogenic foci (calcifications), or valvular regurgitation.</p>
        <p>A mechanical PR interval exceeding the 98th percentile for gestational age (150 ms) or the presence of second-degree AV block indicates the need for intensified monitoring and consideration of potential prenatal intervention. Postnatally, echocardiography is indicated for any infant with prenatal cardiac abnormalities or an abnormal ECG. Some experts also advocate screening echocardiography in otherwise asymptomatic infants with normal ECGs to detect subclinical structural or functional cardiac abnormalities. In infants with confirmed AV block, echocardiography is employed to assess ventricular function, chamber dimensions, valvular competence, and evidence of cardiomyopathy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r42">[42]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r43">[43]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Surveillance</bold>
</p>
        <p>Approximately 2% of AV block cases in NLE are identified postnatally during the neonatal period, underscoring the need for close monitoring of at-risk newborns during the first month of life. Beyond 28 days of age, routine cardiac surveillance is generally not indicated unless a structural or conduction abnormality has been diagnosed. Infants found to have first- or second-degree AV block after birth require ongoing follow-up by a pediatric cardiologist. For those with a history of transient first-degree AV block in utero, both ECG and echocardiography are recommended at&#x000a0;1 year of age. Infants with transient second-degree AV block that resolved before birth should undergo cardiac evaluation within the first&#x000a0;3 months of life to ensure resolution and assess for any late-onset conduction abnormalities.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r44">[44]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r45">[45]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Key Diagnostic Criteria</bold>
</p>
        <p>NLE is established when a neonate exhibits&#x000a0;1 or more characteristic clinical manifestations, including congenital heart block, the classic cutaneous rash, hematologic cytopenias, or hepatic abnormalities, in conjunction with serologic evidence of maternal or neonatal anti-Ro/SSA or anti-La/SSB antibodies. Because maternal IgG autoantibodies can persist in the infant for up to&#x000a0;6 months, testing the neonate&#x02019;s serum primarily reflects maternal antibody status. It is important to note that congenital heart block occurring in an antibody-negative infant does not constitute NLE and should prompt a thorough investigation for alternative etiologies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r46">[46]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25669.s8" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Management of NLE is guided by the specific organ systems involved and the severity of clinical manifestations. Most noncardiac features are self-limited and require supportive care, whereas cardiac involvement often necessitates specialized monitoring and targeted interventions.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Management of Noncardiac Manifestations</bold>
</p>
        <p>Reversible noncardiac features of NLE&#x02014;including cutaneous rash, hematologic cytopenias, and hepatic dysfunction&#x02014;typically resolve spontaneously and are primarily managed with supportive observation. For cutaneous involvement, strict photoprotection is a cornerstone of management. Topical corticosteroids may be employed in selected cases to reduce inflammation, whereas systemic therapies, such as hydroxychloroquine, are generally not indicated in the neonatal period. Persistent cutaneous sequelae, such as telangiectasias, may be addressed later with laser therapy. When hematologic abnormalities are symptomatic, interventions may include blood or platelet transfusions, glucocorticoids, or intravenous immunoglobulin (IVIG), all of which have demonstrated efficacy in selected cases.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r47">[47]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Management of Cardiac Involvement</bold>
</p>
        <p>Cardiac manifestations of NLE, particularly AV&#x000a0;heart block, are associated with substantial morbidity and mortality and necessitate coordinated care by a multidisciplinary team. Management strategies encompass maternal serologic screening, serial fetal echocardiography, and comprehensive parental counseling.</p>
        <p>Pharmacologic interventions:</p>
        <p>Early reports suggested that fluorinated corticosteroids (eg, betamethasone or dexamethasone) could prevent progression of fetal AV block; however, subsequent studies have not demonstrated consistent benefit, and their use carries maternal and fetal risks, including fetal growth restriction and oligohydramnios. IVIG has also been explored for prevention and treatment, but evidence of efficacy remains inconclusive. In contrast, hydroxychloroquine (400 mg daily), when initiated between 6 and 10 weeks&#x02019; gestation, has been associated with a reduced risk of neonatal cardiac lupus, particularly in women with a previously affected child. Beta-adrenergic agonists may transiently increase fetal heart rate and stroke volume but do not prevent the development of AV block.</p>
        <p>Prognostic Indicators:</p>
        <p>Poor fetal prognosis is suggested by a ventricular rate less than 55 beats per minute, hydrops fetalis, cardiomegaly,&#x000a0;AV valve regurgitation, or low aortic flow velocity. Temporary pacing may be employed while awaiting definitive postnatal therapy. Infants with first-degree or stable second-degree AV block may be observed, as conduction abnormalities can improve as maternal antibodies are cleared. Long-term cardiology follow-up is essential, as up to 15% of affected infants may develop late-onset cardiomyopathy despite intervention.</p>
        <p>In Utero Management:</p>
        <p>Therapeutic options for fetal AV block are limited. The primary approach involves intensive monitoring and coordinated follow-up. In cases of fetal compromise or distress, early delivery with immediate access to emergency pacing is indicated.</p>
        <p>Postnatal Therapy:</p>
        <p>Definitive management involves implantation of a permanent pacemaker, with timing guided by the severity of the conduction abnormality and the infant&#x02019;s clinical status.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r48">[48]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;This intervention is most commonly indicated in infants with complete (third-degree) AV block or those with symptomatic bradycardia.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25669.s9" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis of cutaneous manifestations in&#x000a0;NLE includes neonatal rashes not associated with congenital heart block or maternal autoantibodies (anti-Ro/SSA, anti-La/SSB, or anti-U1 RNP). Such rashes encompass acute annular urticaria, tinea corporis, seborrheic dermatitis, annular erythema of childhood, cutis marmorata telangiectatica congenita, Langerhans cell histiocytosis, and certain autoinflammatory syndromes, including:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Chronic atypical neutrophilic dermatosis with lipodystrophy and elevated temperature (CANDLE) syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Autoinflammation and phospholipase C&#x003b3;2&#x02013;associated antibody deficiency and immune dysregulation (APLAID) syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>STING-associated vasculopathy with onset in infancy (SAVI) syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>C1q deficiency</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Other potential causes of fetal bradycardia or heart block include structural or genetic cardiac disorders such as L-transposition of the great arteries, endocardial cushion defects, Holt-Oram syndrome, and idiopathic familial congenital heart block. Accurate differentiation of these structural or genetic etiologies from autoimmune-mediated AV block is critical, as management strategies and prognostic outcomes differ significantly.</p>
        <p>Neonatal sepsis may also present with rash and signs of heart failure, but the timing, morphology, and distribution of the rash generally differ from that seen in NLE. For example, TORCH infections (toxoplasmosis and other infections&#x02014;including syphilis, rubella, cytomegalovirus, and herpes simplex virus) may produce a &#x0201c;blueberry muffin&#x0201d; rash associated with thrombocytopenia.</p>
        <p>A comprehensive maternal history and targeted diagnostic testing are therefore essential to distinguish neonatal lupus from other infectious, structural, genetic, or autoinflammatory conditions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r2">[2]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25669.s10" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The overall prognosis of NLE is favorable in the absence of cardiac involvement. Cutaneous, hepatic, and hematologic abnormalities generally resolve spontaneously within the first year of life without residual sequelae. In contrast, congenital heart block is associated with significantly increased morbidity and mortality, with reported fetal or neonatal mortality rates of approximately 15% to 30%, particularly in cases complicated by hydrops fetalis or cardiomyopathy. Timely pacemaker implantation substantially improves outcomes, with long-term survival exceeding 90%. With appropriate cardiac monitoring and intervention, most affected children achieve normal neurodevelopmental outcomes and quality of life.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r49">[49]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r50">[50]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25669.s11" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>NLE may be associated with the following complications:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Permanent congenital heart block necessitating lifelong pacemaker dependence</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Endocardial fibroelastosis and myocardial fibrosis</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Progressive cardiomyopathy</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Persistent cutaneous changes, including scarring or telangiectasia</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Rare neurodevelopmental delays, typically secondary to chronic fetal or neonatal hypoperfusion</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25669.s12" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Educating families about NLE is essential to alleviating anxiety and facilitating effective management. Parents should be reassured that NLE is typically a transient condition, resulting from maternal autoantibodies temporarily present in the infant, and is not usually permanent. The majority of neonates with cutaneous, hematologic, or hepatic manifestations resolve completely within 6 to 12 months without lasting sequelae.</p>
        <p>While congenital heart block represents a permanent cardiac complication, it can be effectively managed with pacemaker implantation. Families should receive guidance on skin care, with a particular emphasis on strict photoprotection during early infancy, and clear instructions for recognizing early signs of heart failure, such as poor feeding, lethargy, or respiratory distress, to ensure timely intervention.</p>
        <p>Ongoing follow-up with pediatric cardiology and other relevant specialists is critical for monitoring and long-term management. Mothers should be counseled about the risk of recurrence in future pregnancies and the potential protective role of hydroxychloroquine in significantly reducing it. With multidisciplinary care and vigilant surveillance, the long-term prognosis for affected infants is generally favorable.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25669.s13" sec-type="Pearls and Other Issues">
        <title>Pearls and Other Issues</title>
        <p>Key points&#x000a0;to keep in mind about NLE include the following:&#x000a0;</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>NLE results from the transplacental transfer of maternal autoantibodies, specifically anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>It is most commonly associated with maternal SLE or Sj&#x000f6;gren syndrome, although mothers may be clinically asymptomatic.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The most serious and characteristic complication is congenital heart block, particularly third-degree AV block.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Additional manifestations include cutaneous rash, hematologic cytopenias (anemia, neutropenia, thrombocytopenia), and elevated hepatic enzymes.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The cutaneous rash is photosensitive, often presenting as annular erythematous plaques that typically involve the periorbital region, producing the classic &#x0201c;raccoon-eye&#x0201d; appearance.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Cardiac manifestations generally develop between 18 and 24 weeks of gestation and may present as fetal bradycardia.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Maternal or neonatal serologic testing for anti-Ro/SSA and anti-La/SSB antibodies confirms the diagnosis; these antibodies persist in the infant for 6 to 8 months.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>The majority of noncardiac manifestations are transient, resolving within 6 to 12 months as maternal antibodies are cleared.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Congenital heart block is permanent and frequently requires pacemaker implantation.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Maternal hydroxychloroquine therapy during pregnancy has been shown to reduce the risk of cardiac NLE in subsequent pregnancies.</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25669.s14" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Identifying pregnancies at risk for NLE requires coordinated, multidisciplinary care involving obstetricians, pediatricians, pediatric cardiologists, neonatologists, nursing staff, and other perinatal specialists. In pregnant women with SLE or positive anti-Ro/SSA or anti-La/SSB antibodies, individualized care plans should be prioritized to optimize maternal and fetal outcomes.</p>
        <p>Current prenatal surveillance guidelines emphasize early identification of at-risk fetuses to facilitate timely therapeutic intervention. The American Heart Association recommends initiating fetal echocardiographic screening at 16 weeks&#x02019; gestation, followed by weekly or biweekly assessments until 28 weeks for women who are SSA/SSB antibody&#x02013;positive. In women with a history of a previously affected child, weekly surveillance from 16 to 28 weeks&#x02019; gestation is advised to monitor for early cardiac manifestations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r29">[29]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-25669.r51">[51]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-25669.s15">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>
